  "verdict": "Naive Bayes' conditional-independence assumption is violated here. Its evidence is multiplied once per *feature* instead of once per *fact*, so 99.1% of its predictions land below 0.01 or above 0.99 (logistic: 81.8%) and its mean |log-odds| is 26.6 against logistic's 8.0. The cost shows up in log-loss (0.3438 vs 0.0540, 6.4\u00d7 worse) more than in ECE (0.0298 vs 0.0273), because ECE averages over bins and most of its mass is in the same two bins. The ablation isolates the double-counting: dropping one feature from each strongly correlated pair gives log-loss 0.2071, while dropping the same number of *uncorrelated* features gives 0.5198. Removing redundant evidence helps more than removing independent evidence, which is what a double-count looks like."
}